  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Find colored rectangles\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    colors = defaultdict(list)\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] != 0:\n                colors[grid[r][c]].append((r, c))\n    \n    rects = {}\n    for color, cells in colors.items():\n        min_r = min(r for r, c in cells)\n        max_r = max(r for r, c in cells)\n        min_c = min(c for r, c in cells)\n        max_c = max(c for r, c in cells)\n        h = max_r - min_r + 1\n        w = max_c - min_c + 1\n        rects[color] = (min_r, min_c, h, w)\n    \n    # Each rect has area h*w. Largest area stays, moved to touch bottom edge but shifted up by its size.\n    # Actually let me look at the pattern more carefully.\n    \n    # Sort by area descending\n    sorted_colors = sorted(rects.keys(), key=lambda c: rects[c][2] * rects[c][3], reverse=True)\n    \n    # The largest rect shifts up so its bottom is where its top was (roughly)\n    # The smallest rect shifts down and is surrounded by empty space\n    \n    # Looking at examples: each block keeps its column position but shifts vertically.\n    # The shift amount seems related to the block's size.\n    # Each block shifts up by its height, and the smallest block goes below everything.\n    \n    # Actually: each rectangle is moved up by its own height (keeping columns), and wrapped/centered.\n    # Let me check Ex1: color1 was rows 11-14, h=4. Moved to 7-10 (shifted up by 4). color4 was 11-14 h=4, moved to 7-10 (up 4). color2 was 13-14 h=2, moved to 11-12 (up 2).\n    \n    # So each rectangle shifts up by its own height.\n    \n    result = [[0]*cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    \n    for color, (min_r, min_c, h, w) in rects.items():\n        new_min_r = min_r - h\n        for r in range(h):\n            for c in range(w):\n                nr = new_min_r + r\n                nc = min_c + c\n                if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols:\n                    result[nr][nc] = color\n    \n    return result",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    rows, cols = len(grid), len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Find colored rectangles\n    from collections import defaultdict\n    colors = defaultdict(list)\n    for r in range(rows):\n        for c in range(cols):\n            if grid[r][c] != 0:\n                colors[grid[r][c]].append((r, c))\n    \n    rects = {}\n    for color, cells in colors.items():\n        min_r = min(r for r, c in cells)\n        max_r = max(r for r, c in cells)\n        min_c = min(c for r, c in cells)\n        max_c = max(c for r, c in cells)\n        h = max_r - min_r + 1\n        w = max_c - min_c + 1\n        rects[color] = (min_r, min_c, h, w)\n    \n    # Each rect has area h*w. Largest area stays, moved to touch bottom edge but shifted up by its size.\n    # Actually let me look at the pattern more carefully.\n    \n    # Sort by area descending\n    sorted_colors = sorted(rects.keys(), key=lambda c: rects[c][2] * rects[c][3], reverse=True)\n    \n    # The largest rect shifts up so its bottom is where its top was (roughly)\n    # The smallest rect shifts down and is surrounded by empty space\n    \n    # Looking at examples: each block keeps its column position but shifts vertically.\n    # The shift amount seems related to the block's size.\n    # Each block shifts up by its height, and the smallest block goes below everything.\n    \n    # Actually: each rectangle is moved up by its own height (keeping columns), and wrapped/centered.\n    # Let me check Ex1: color1 was rows 11-14, h=4. Moved to 7-10 (shifted up by 4). color4 was 11-14 h=4, moved to 7-10 (up 4). color2 was 13-14 h=2, moved to 11-12 (up 2).\n    \n    # So each rectangle shifts up by its own height.\n    \n    result = [[0]*cols for _ in range(rows)]\n    \n    for color, (min_r, min_c, h, w) in rects.items():\n        new_min_r = min_r - h\n        for r in range(h):\n            for c in range(w):\n                nr = new_min_r + r\n                nc = min_c + c\n                if 0 <= nr < rows and 0 <= nc < cols:\n                    result[nr][nc] = color\n    \n    return result",
